  "slug": "models-agent-level-3abc",
  "summary": "Surrogate and hybrid frameworks now represent agent- and subpopulation-level variation while attributing stochastic noise in disease simulations.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how simulation models of disease and structured populations represent heterogeneity and stochasticity. Conditional tabular GANs emulate road-injury agents in a multi-disease agent-based model, cutting run-time 86.6% while retaining dynamics. LCPTA combines ODEs with latent class trajectories, implemented in the TemporalMixtureModels.jl package, to model multiscale heterogeneous disease progression. A published global sensitivity analysis separates noise channels in a stochastic yellow fever spillover model. What is new is the use of learned or mixture-based components to represent agent- or subpopulation-level variation alongside formal attribution of stochastic variability. Two other preprints extend the theme: a frequency-domain approach makes measurement likelihoods approximately Gaussian for reaction network identification, and PINN-based inference recovers size-structured fish oogenesis functions from cross-sectional data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-07"
}
